.TH CCOL_BEGIN 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_begin \- begin iteration over any supported container
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

cmap_iterator *ccol_begin(container);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro gives an iterator at the first element of container. It selects
the correct begin_iter function at compile time. It uses _Generic on the
type of container. The supported types are cvec, chmap and cbmap.
A container of any other type, a void * included, is a compile error.

The macro gives NULL, and no error, when the container is empty. A
container handle that is NULL iterates as an empty container. Therefore, the
macro gives NULL for it, and no error.
It calls ccol_fatal_err() when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
container  A cvec, chmap or cbmap variable.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
cmap_iterator* at the first element, or NULL when the container is empty.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Use ccol_iter_declare(container, it) before a call to ccol_begin in a for
loop. That macro gives you typed accessors for the iterator.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_iter_declare(vec, it);
for (it = ccol_begin(vec); it != NULL; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("[%zu] = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                            *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_for_each (3),
.BR ccol_end (3)
